  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe goal is to minimize Erdos's minimum overlap integral for a step function h on [0,2] with integral constrained to 1. Given the non-convex nature of the problem, we need a robust global optimization method to escape local minima. Traditional local methods often get stuck around the parent solution (331 grid points). We combine multi-resolution optimization, Bayesian optimization (GP) for regression and acquisition, and hybridization with local optimizers.\n\nKey improvements over previous local-only strategies:\n1. **Multi-resolution approach**: Explores solutions at dimensions 139, 331, and 983 to balance grid size and optimization complexity.\n2. **Bayesian optimization**: Uses Gaussian Process regression to model the objective surface, enabling efficient global exploration.\n3. **Adaptive hybridization**: Switches between Bayesian exploration and local refinement based on error reduction.\n4. **Expectile-based objective**: Employs a rho-convex surrogate to approximate the mode='full' correlation.\n\nThis strategy should overcome the shallow global exploration and local minima issues of previous SLSQP-based approaches while respecting the parent solution.\n\nMathematical specification\nVariables:\nh \u2208 \u211d^n (function values on grid points)\n\nConstraints:\n0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 \u2200i (box constraints)\nsum(h) = n/2 (integral constraint)\n\nObjective: \nMinimize max_k \u27e8h, 1 - \u03c3h * shift_k\u27e9 where \u03c3h is the step function, shift_k is the lag-k kernel, and \u27e8\u22c5\u27e9 denotes discrete correlation with dx=2/n.\n\nEqual to:\nminimize (1/dx) * max_k (correlation(h, 1-h)[k]) with dx=2/n\n\nUse rho-convex (scaled L1) surrogate for smoothness and coregionalization for correlated evaluations.\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Input validation:\n- Grid sizes: 139, 331, 983\n- Ensure initial_h_values provided (n=331) is projected to target grid\n\n2. Phase initialization:\n- Initialize GP regressor with kernel=RBF+Matern\n- Set initial population from parent solutions with noise perturbations\n\n3. Core optimization loop:\n   while time_left > 250 seconds and improvement_threshold not met:\n   - Fit GP model to current candidates\n   - Score acquisition (expected improvement, gp_hedge)\n   - Generate new candidates at top acquisition points\n   - Hybridize: 80% Bayesian candidates, 20% random/SLSQP neighbors\n\n4. Objective function (eq_balance objectives):\ndef rho_convex(x):\n   h_clip = np.clip(x, 0, 1)\n   total = np.sum(h_clip)\n   adjusted = h_clip + (n/2 - total)/n  # To enforce integral constraint\n   adjusted = np.clip(adjusted, 0, 1)\n   corr = np.correlate(adjusted, 1-adjusted, mode='full')\n   return (2/n) * np.max(corr)  [This is the actual normalized goal]\n\n5. Constraints enforcement:\ndef project_feasible(x):\n   transforms x to [0,1] with sum(n/2) using iterative projection: \n   - Clip boundaries\n   - Adjust remaining sum uniformly using active set\n\n6. Dimension transfer (if grid changes):\n- Coarse to fine: Zero-phase interpolation preserving integral\n- Fine to coarse: Simple averaging with parent re-projection\n\n7. Termination:\n- Minimum improvement threshold 1e-6\n- Budget expiration\n- Integral constraint failure\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n1. Objective cycling:\n   - Rho-convex (L1-based)\n   - Smooth entropy (KL-divergence)\n   - Noise-tolerant (Huberized correlation)\n\n2. Acquisition functions:\n   - Expected improvement (default)\n   - Probability improvement with noise (1e-2)\n   - Maximum improvement (10% of time)\n\n3. Dimension progression:\n   - Ascending order (small -> large)\n   - Descending (reverse exploration)\n   - Adaptive based on feasibility (inconclusive in parent grids)\n\nComputational budget and robustness:\n- Time allocation: 70% exploration, 30% refinement\n- Warm start all grids from parent\n- Graceful degradation: Return parent if any dimension fails\n- Multi-fidelity scoring: Lower-dimensional grids provide initial mapping\n\nValidation and return contract\n1. Feasibility checks:\n   - np.all(0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1)\n   - abs(np.sum(h) - n/2) < 1e-3\n2. Objective consistency:\n   - Compute_c5(h) matches surrogate within 5e-4\n   - NaN-free correlation results\n3. Dimension restoration:\n   - If grid changed, re-evaluate candidate using final grid\n4. Fallback: Return initial_h_values if:\n   - Budget exceeded\n   - No improvement in final grid\n   - Memory overflow during Fourier analysis\n\nThe algorithm transfers between resolution levels using guardrails to ensure valid function representation and continuity in the step function properties over the domain.",
